// @reject: an export block belongs INSIDE a module block. `#> { … }` written at
// @reject: file top level, with no enclosing `# name { … }`, is not Zymbol: both
// @reject: Rust parsers refuse it at parse time with `unexpected token:
// @reject: ExportBlock`, and so does `zymbol check`.
// @reject: The browser engine accepts it and runs the file. Running this module
// @reject: on its own it emits only `warning: unused variable 'publica'`; loaded
// @reject: by 03_file_level_export_block.zy it exports the name and prints 14,
// @reject: where the other two engines refuse to parse the module at all.
// @reject: The evidence that the Rust engines are right is the code that exists:
// @reject: of the 83 module files across the six LDV applications, 83 write the
// @reject: export block inside `# name { … }` and none at file level.
// @reject: This is the JS-permissive direction, which is the worse one — a module
// @reject: written in the playground runs there and fails to parse in the CLI.
// @reject: Same class as 01_import_after_code.zy (DM-12): a rule both Rust
// @reject: parsers always enforced, written down nowhere, that the browser engine
// @reject: never checked. Found 2026-08-31 during the documentation audit.
#> {
    publica
}

TOPE := 7

privada() {
    <~ TOPE * 2
}

publica() {
    <~ privada()
}
